Step 1 - Verify if it's HTTP

Open CMD and run

netstat -ano | findstr 9823

Then

telnet 198.18.5.165 9823

(or PowerShell)

Test-NetConnection 198.18.5.165 -Port 9823
Step 2 - Check if it is .NET

Run

sigcheck.exe SumoGUI.exe

or

strings SumoGUI.exe | findstr /i "System.ServiceModel"

If you find

System.ServiceModel
BasicHttpBinding
NetTcpBinding
ChannelFactory

then it's WCF.

Step 3 - Find the Configuration File (Very Important)

Using Process Monitor, filter:

Process Name is SumoGUI.exe

Watch for

ReadFile

Look for

app.config
SumoGUI.exe.config
*.config
*.xml

Open the config file.

You're looking for something like:

<endpoint
address="net.tcp://198.18.5.165:9823/SumoService"
binding="netTcpBinding"/>

or

<endpoint
address="http://198.18.5.165:9823/SumoService"
binding="basicHttpBinding"/>

This tells you exactly what protocol is used.

Step 4 - Check the Loaded DLLs

Using Process Explorer

Double-click SumoGUI.exe

Go to

DLLs

Search for

System.ServiceModel.dll

or

System.Net.Http.dll

or

PresentationFramework

If System.ServiceModel.dll is loaded, it's almost certainly WCF.

Step 5 - Check the Connection in Wireshark (Best Method)

Even if you don't have other commercial tools, Wireshark is free. If you're allowed to install it, it's extremely useful.

Capture traffic on port 9823.

If you see:

POST
HTTP
SOAPAction

it's HTTP/SOAP.

If you see unreadable binary frames immediately after TCP establishment, it's likely net.tcp or a proprietary protocol.

Step 6 - Use WinDbg

Attach to the process:

windbg -pn SumoGUI.exe

Set breakpoints:

bp ws2_32!connect
bp ws2_32!send
bp ws2_32!recv

When a breakpoint hits:

k

If the stack contains

System.ServiceModel

or

clr

it's .NET/WCF.

Step 7 - Capture with Burp

If the application uses HTTP/SOAP, Burp can capture it.

Configure Windows proxy:

127.0.0.1:8080

If nothing appears:

It may ignore the system proxy.
It may use net.tcp, which Burp cannot intercept.
It may connect directly via sockets.
